export const TIDEWE_REFERENCE_APPEARANCE_GUIDANCE = `This appearance-evidence rule applies to every outdoor product category, including items with an unset or unfamiliar category. Current reference images and their visualObservations are evidence of directly visible appearance and spatial relationships. A clearly visible component's shape, material boundaries, texture, layout, location, attachment and contact relationships do not need a separate confirmed performance fact ID. Use that evidence for useful overall views, close-ups and ordinary contact already supported by the references; do not hide a visible component or replace its close-up with presenter commentary merely because specifications are unconfirmed. Missing performance evidence does not make a visibly documented component or its location unconfirmed.
Let each reference image determine what to describe. Give the actual focal subject of every detail reference priority, even when an overall image or earlier summary did not mention it. Name the visible part freely from the image and describe its shown shape, surface boundaries, layout and relationship to the surrounding product or visible contact. There is no fixed component checklist or whitelist: an unfamiliar or unlisted detail is equally valid evidence. Category examples are optional context, never a required inventory or a limit on which reference-visible parts may be shown. Do not replace the depicted local detail with a more familiar example, invent an absent component or require a hand or control in the frame. For an isolated detail, preserve the relationships actually visible in that image without guessing where it attaches outside the frame.
A pictured battery or accessory can be described as visible in the reference, without claiming it is included with purchase. Confirmed facts are still required for functionality, settings, operating sequences, measured outcomes and purchase conditions; a visible part, icon, contact or surface detail alone does not establish those claims. Preserve shown structure instead of inventing hidden parts or a new operation. Write the actual supported picture and action directly in visual fields. Keep uncertainty notes and policy commentary such as “未经确认”, “待核实” or “不特写” out of the storyboard prose. When a detail is genuinely not visible, choose another supported view and describe that view positively.`;

